#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125423} :activity/id #uuid "165fbf2b-e87c-54f7-8870-018acdd52972" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048195 :topic/id #uuid "87752628-b947-5399-88e8-72bed0a3c112" :topic/math-academy-id 1970} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125792 :tutorial/content "An $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is said to be diagonalizable if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, the matrix ![](images/81/810ae901dcd83f59297a122fbf20beb91e29374baff41c19bcacf40b3a7e7091.svg) is diagonalizable because\n![](images/2c/2c41221da0e4c0895928e65602e588f380452172e29d79f6e9f68ebb49e57807.svg)\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*An $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is diagonalizable if and only if there exists a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that consists of eigenvectors of $A$.*\n\nSince eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are linearly independent, we can also  conclude the following fact:\n\n*If an $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ has $n$ *distinct* eigenvalues (that is, $n$ simple roots of the corresponding characteristic equation), then there exists a basis of eigenvectors. Thus, the matrix is diagonalizable.*\n\nLet's show you how to use the diagonalization theorem with an example. \n\nSuppose that $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-2},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=-3$ are eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$  matrix $A$, and that\n![](images/ec/ecc57b51a49c8d1d6fbadd69908dbf6f48803cd6ca1f72ad189a1e5410c5d71c.svg)\nare the respective eigenvectors. To find an invertible matrix $P$ and  a diagonal matrix $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$  we do the following: .\n\nFirst, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/4f/4f878ce6a4b6f770cd520e22a8e4a3c6f8b3012b7de446bd3f533e61bda85a13.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-2},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/95/95c896911a6d54ea6385821846a3f0e4fc1a8f05f29e83b951ba270a3898938e.svg)\n\n**Note:** If the matrix $A$ does not have $n$ distinct eigenvalues, it is not always possible to diagonalize it. However, the algorithm to diagonalize any diagonalizable $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix is the same as the $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ case!" :tutorial/id #uuid "aef11f46-9f49-598e-9d5b-938d54814c1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3578 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2b97df2a-2c81-5e5c-aae7-42dd0059d96e" :step/math-academy-id 20125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124341 :question/id #uuid "a285ae3e-4f06-5e09-a06f-b2620be99888" :question/math-academy-id "e-5504"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459271}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "c9398a9c-468d-5eda-8f0e-08b6ae582dd6" :step/math-academy-id 20126 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 175